{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792174323000-reviewer-6db-diff-glm.glm-5-3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-16 19:15:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-17 02:15:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: glm\nSession-ID: e33f308d-2ca8-4019-917d-4c85854673b4\n# Task: diff review of slice 6d-b — glm glm-5.3 verdict (resumed thread)\nRole: Architect\nImplementers:\n- Model: glm-5.3 | Assigned: 2026-10-17 01:52 +07 | Status: completed | Rationale: non-author review of codex-authored 6d-b; final message promoted verbatim below (two CLI warning lines dropped); stdout at collab/1792174323000-reviewer-6db-diff-glm.glm-5-3.stdout.log\n\n6DB-DIFF-REVIEW: APPROVE-WITH-NITS\n\n# Diff review of slice 6d-b — glm (non-author reviewer), read-only\n\nRead completely: the full diff (committed follow-up 262fc609d + the uncommitted slice), the engineer's report, v2.1 §3.3/§3.4 and the 6d-b change list, and my own 6d-a watch items. Verification was source-level throughout; the JVM claims (quick tier 4,090/269,314/0, focused 283/7,875/0) are the engineer's and match the code's shape.\n\n## (a) Fidelity to v2.1's 6d-b list and the four outcomes — held\n\n- **`:fuel` contract**: `check-common!` validates nil-or-positive-integer (rejects 0/-1/1.5/\"1\"/false, retains nil/1/10 — pinned in `fuel-configuration-and-repeated-boundaries-test`), retained by the constructor and `reopen-config`; execution is `(if fuel (sr/run-fuel m0 fuel) (vm/run m0))` under the untouched gate/halted guards. No auto-arm: `cycle-result` adds `:yin.k/at-boundary true` only when control is set, `hand-off` stays explicit, and no policy clause was added.\n- **Stage-local channel (P2-8)**: `stage-result` checks tenure against its *input*, runs the operation, and on a convertible failure returns `{:status :failed :state <input> :failure …}` — the stage-local catch closes over the stage's input, so a later tenure failure carries the previous stage's completed machine, never `step-active`'s original argument; journal-uncertain/inbox-refusal rethrow; `failed-stage` consumes via `end-run` on the carried state. The four stages (execution/replay/emit/read) thread successors through `completed-stage`; the post-cycle drain is separately stage-guarded; a throwing unreturned stage contributes nothing. The old pre-fix behavior is now visibly enshrined-nowhere — see judgment call 2.\n- **Four outcomes**: (1) live-tenure preemption with `:at-boundary` ✓; (2) tenure-after-slice with `:yin.k/retained-boundary` (pc/segment/checkpoint/steps) assoc'd in `end-run`, inspection-only — `(:bytes result)` pinned unchanged and the recovering holder's machine asserted free of any retained marker or control ✓; (3) expiry recovers the last durable checkpoint — the clock-cuts test recovers the original checkpoint bytes, and Stage E's `running-expiry` pins recovery at checkpoint pc 0 against a progressed pc > 0 ✓; (4) fenced reopen — `running-products-rehydrate-byte-for-byte-and-refuse-execution-test` (both products: running+waits, running+ready) reproduces the pack byte-for-byte, retains the ready queue outside the scheduler, refuses all four execution entries, refuses the uncertain-offer abort, zero effects.\n- **Export/recovery**: `first-refusal` deleted; `enter` moves the root's ordered queue into the record (a child's queue rides in the pack via the 6d-a carrier, with `recovery-metadata`'s child fallback carrying it too); `kind-of` is control→running then parked then waits∪ready→blocked; `valid-state?` checks `:ready-count` against slot 18 (with a decode tamper case); fenced extraction consumes `:yin.k/recovery-ready`; `abort`'s conditions are untouched and reinstatement is pinned to exact-machine equality with zero calls.\n- **Custody (b)**: `ledger.cljc:277` and `grant.cljc:74-75` admit `:running`, `:halted` refusal intact in both sets; the running-offerable tests pin offer-committed and the projection baseline kind. Zero-past-the-bound is asserted at every cut: nine clock cuts and three export cuts each assert zero serves/attaches/observations (export cuts additionally zero non-release requests and no `:yin.k/resumed`), and Stage E's expiry snapshot asserts `{serves 0 attaches 0 observations 0}` with empty inbound input/admit, side and targets. No execution guard was introduced in 6d-b — the 6d-a guard is reused and integration-tested.\n\n## (c) Assertions and mutants — no weakening found\n\nEvery cut asserts whole-machine equality (`(= ended (:machine result))`, and Stage E's `(= (assoc before :yin.k/gate :ended) (:machine ended))` — the strongest form), plus pinned checkpoint address, steps-since-checkpoint, unchanged bytes, gate refusal on the ended machine, and non-adoption by the recoverer. The 13-mutant table covers each new rule with real kill counts (failure-reverts-to-input 18/0, expired-as-holding 57/0, gate-unchecked 25/0, ready-reinstatement 13/0, …); the campaign predates two later assertion additions with implementation rules unchanged since — acceptable as disclosed. The one replaced legacy expectation is judgment call 2 below and is a strengthening, not a weakening. The deleted `a-non-empty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent-test` is the plan's own §3.8-authorized replacement (`first-refusal` no longer exists).\n\n## (d) Docs — faithful\n\nUCF §7.7.4's Quiesce step 1 is replaced verbatim with the completed-outcome retention text; §7.7.4's step 3, holds (\"not export-ready\", no fixed cycle count), §7.7.6, §7.8 (title, steps 1/3/9), §7.10's no-layer-collapsing row, §7.11/7.11.1's renamed harness and boundary wording, and the obsolete child-quiescence paragraph all match v2.1's owned text; continuation §7.3 restructures the four outcomes with the stage-outcome map verbatim; linker-DHT §14.1/14.2 hunks land v2 §3.7.6's replacements. The old §7.6.3 not-quiescent sentence survives as immediately-superseded historical text with no runtime producer left — the right disposition. §3.8's checklist holds (the guard is documented as shipping with 6d-a).\n\n## Rulings on the two judgment calls\n\n1. **`steps-since-checkpoint` unit = completed holder slices — CONFIRMED.** The plan's `k` never had a defined unit, and no honest guest-instruction count exists to derive: `run-fuel` exposes no completed-instruction counter, a slice can park early (fewer than n instructions), and a scheduler round spreads the budget over a tree of tasks — deriving a count from fuel would be exactly the kind of invented number v2.1 §4 forbids. The diagnostic is inspection-only, monotone over the holder's life (accurate, since `:bytes` is fixed for the holder's life — see nit 3), pinned per cut (0 for `:before`, 1 after a completed slice), and the continuation §7.3 text states the unit explicitly (\"holder program steps, not a guest-instruction count\"). This is the right design call.\n2. **Strengthened bare-effect tenure test — CONFIRMED as required, not optional.** Under the ruled latest-completed-stage semantics the retained machine after an emit-stage tenure loss is the post-sweep machine, and the sweep retires a halted install child in the sweep it halts in — so the old child-presence expectation encoded the pre-P2-8 bug (retaining the pre-slice machine). The replacement — exact equality with `replay(vm/run machine)` gated `:ended`, computed independently in the test — is strictly stronger and matches the plan's \"assert the exact retained machine\" instruction; the one-permitted-write assertion is unchanged.\n\n## Findings (all P3; nothing blocking)\n\n1. **P3 — Dart loses the cut matrices.** `clock-cuts…` and `running-export-tenure-cuts…` are `#?(:cljd nil :default …)` (correctly `:cljd`-first), so the Dart lane's exact-retention evidence is Stage E's `running-expiry` plus the non-redefs tests (products, queues, hand-off, expiry-refusal, holds). Acceptable under the established gating discipline, but the landing report should state it explicitly rather than let \"quick tier green on three hosts\" imply the nine cuts ran everywhere.\n2. **P3 — defensive `:resources` normalization is unexplained.** `clock-cuts…`'s `mark` resets `expected`'s `:resources` to m0's with no comment; the guarded-handle round trip (`guard-writes`→`restore-handles`) should already make them identical, so either the normalization is dead (drop it) or there is a live case (name it in a comment). One line.\n3. **P3 — `:completed-slices` has no reset and no comment.** It is accurate because a holder's checkpoint (`:bytes`) is fixed for its life, but a future re-checkpointing feature would silently mislabel the count. Add the one-line invariant comment at the `update` in `run-cycle`'s execution stage.\n4. **P3 — my 6d-a watch items, status:** the `:store-keys` drift caveat is now a known-gaps row (P3, harness slice) ✓; the 2×-lift preflight cost now also applies to every holder export attempt (`export/encode`'s lift path) — no gate asserts export latency, so this is a Fable-awareness item only, worth a line in the 6e brief if export frequency grows; the with-redefs/Dart discipline is correctly followed in the two new redefs sites.\n\n## What Fable's gate and the landing lanes should watch\n\n- **Fable**: the two confirmed judgment calls above (both change documented semantics or strengthen a legacy pin — exactly the kind of diff to eyeball); the `receive-module` restore behavior and this slice's `:yin.k/admitted-modules` compose forwarding interacting on the running-install-child hand-off (tested in `exclusive-fuel-boundary-and-running-install-child-handoff-test`, but it is the newest seam); and that `recovery-metadata` gained `:ready-count` without a version bump — fine for holder-local objects under the no-compatibility-promise rule, but worth a conscious nod.\n- **Landing lanes**: Node first (the two cut tests are Node's to run; commit 51117e93's silent-`with-redefs` precedent means a pin failure like \"0 measured\" is the failure signature to look for); Dart second (expect the gated tests to be *absent*, not green — check the log shows them skipped and Stage E's running scenarios present, including the process-matrix `running-boundary-handoff-and-expiry-across-processes-test` which has not been executed by anyone yet); then the three-host slow tier, budgeting Dart generously given Stage E's 8.5 s JVM measurement and this subsystem's slow-lane history. Cross-host: the Stage E scenario set (`running-expiry`, three `running-handoff` cuts) must produce identical results on every host — any divergence is a finding, not a fixture.\n\n**Bottom line:** a faithful, well-evidenced implementation of v2.1's 6d-b list and all four outcomes, with the strongest-form equality assertions at every cut and an honest mutant campaign; the two judgment calls are correct and I confirm both. Approve with the four P3 nits; Fable's sign-off remains the merge gate.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
